00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.702
Welcome to Multi-vector Image Retrieval,

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.771
built in partnership with Qdrant.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:08.575
In this course, you'll learn how multi-vector image retrieval works

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:12.779
and best practices for using it in your AI applications.

00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:14.948
For a long time, it was easiest

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:17.550
to give LLMs access to only text data,

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:20.754
but that leaves a lot of useful information inaccessible

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.758
because it's stored in images and other forms of multimodal data.

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:30.597
This course focuses on a collection of image retrieval techniques called Multi-vector.

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:34.968
In contrast, standard vector retrieval works by representing each document or image

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:37.670
with a single large vector.

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:39.939
An image is retrieved if their vector

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:41.775
is a good match for a query.

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:44.944
Multi-vector techniques use many smaller vectors

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:49.649
to represent the meaning of, say, smaller patches of an image.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:52.819
storing this detailed vector information

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:55.121
then allows for fine-grained matching

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.323
between the tokens in the text query

00:00:57.323 --> 00:01:01.027
and the image patches that make up your image or document.

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:04.931
This approach to provide high quality image search

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:09.836
that works well even on complex documents that combine text, images, slides,

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:12.439
and storing all this vector data

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:15.308
does lead to challenges with memory usage and search.

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:18.411
But some new techniques address these problems

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:21.414
and you'll explore these solutions as well in this course.

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:24.818
To help you to understand these Multi-vector image retrieval techniques,

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:27.620
I'm delighted to introduce your instructor for this course,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.224
Kacper Łukawski, who is an experienced AI engineer

00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:33.626
and a developer advocate for the vector database

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:36.229
company Qdrant. Thanks, Andrew.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:39.432
You will first learn the fundamentals of multi-vector retrieval

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.534
by seeing how it works for text.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:45.438
Then, you will see how this approach is adapted for image retrieval

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:47.440
by a model called ColPali,

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:50.477
and some techniques for optimizing its performance.

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:54.481
Finally, you will explore how an approach called MUVERA

00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:59.185
aims to combine the best qualities of multi-vector and single-vector image retrieval.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:02.789
You will learn the foundational concepts underlying each method.

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:07.694
and then we'll implement them inside of a functioning multimodal RAG system.

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:10.430
You can expect a nice blend of

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.899
conceptual and practical topics throughout this course.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:14.934
There's been a lot of excitement

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:17.003
around the industry about image retrieval broadly

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:19.706
and specifically multi-vector techniques.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:22.609
Most of the LLMs that people are using today

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:25.879
are actually VLMs or vision language models.

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:30.083
They are just as capable of processing images as text.

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:33.086
The challenge has been building system that can accurately

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:35.788
and efficiently retrieve image data

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:38.091
to take advantage of that capability.

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:42.095
The techniques in this course, like ColPali or MUVERA,

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:44.797
are finally doing that. I really think

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:47.333
this is the future of this field.

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:49.469
and that will see it powering

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:51.638
many exciting applications in the coming years.

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:54.140
Many people have worked to create this course.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.645
From DeepLearning.AI, GT Wrobel contributed to its development.

00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:02.649
In the first lesson, you get familiar with multi-vector retrieval

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:04.617
by getting hands on with ColBERT,

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:08.621
which is a widely used multi-vector approach for text retrieval.

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:12.325
Join Kacper in the next video, and let's get started.
